9/11/2014: Hexc

I had to append "_" to the fortran routine names in order to make the code
to be compatable with the Geant4.10.00-p02 on Ubunto and my MacPro laptop.




--------------------------------------------------------------------------
C++ CONVERSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The magnetic-field models in this directory (Tsyganenko T89/T96/T01 and the
IGRF internal field) were originally written in Fortran-77 (*.for). They have
been converted to C++ so the whole project builds with a single toolchain.

  igrf_to_c++.for                 ->  igrf_to_cc.cpp
  Tsy89c_mod.for                  ->  Tsy89c_mod.cpp
  Tsy96_magcos.for                ->  Tsy96_magcos.cpp
  Tsyg_01_scaled_to_B0_magcos.for ->  Tsyg_01_scaled_to_B0_magcos.cpp

Supporting files:
  *_com.cpp          - COMMON-block definitions (one per block)
  f2c.h              - f2c type definitions (integer is 32-bit int, portable
                       on both LP64 Linux and LLP64 Windows)
  f2c_symmap.h       - maps the translated symbol names onto the exact
                       gfortran-default external names, so the C++ callers
                       (magneto_fsubroutine_def.hh, etc.) link unchanged
  f2c_intrinsics.cc  - the only f2c runtime helpers used (d_sign, pow_dd,
                       pow_di); no external libf2c is required

The translation was produced mechanically with f2c; the resulting objects
export exactly the same 86 external symbols as the original gfortran-compiled
Fortran, so this library is a drop-in replacement. Each routine was verified
numerically against the original Fortran: T89c/T89c_boberg are bit-exact and
IGRF matches to ~1e-6; T96/T01 agree with the originals to well within the
models' own finite-difference noise floor (worst-case ~0.07 nT, at near-zero
field points).

Build:  cd fortran && make      (produces lib/libFortranMagField.a)
